            High Atlas mountain pass N9: spectacular vistas coupled with high hairpin frequency and sheer cliff edges.
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3>Hazard 1: The Tizi n'Tichka Mountain Pass (Elevation 2,260m)</h3>
        <p>
          To reach Ouarzazate, the Dadès Valley, or the Sahara Desert from Marrakech, you must cross the High Atlas Mountains via the famous <strong>N9 road over the Tizi n'Tichka pass</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          While major widening projects have improved sections of the road, Tichka remains an intensely demanding alpine route:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Overwhelming Switchbacks:</strong> Continuous hairpin turns with sheer vertical drops on the unbarricaded outer edge.</li>
          <li><strong>Heavy Commercial Freight:</strong> Massive phosphate and quarry semi-trucks crawl uphill at 15 km/h, spewing black exhaust. Passing them requires squeezing onto narrow shoulders or timing overtakes around blind mountain bends.</li>
          <li><strong>Unpredictable Weather:</strong> Between November and April, Tizi n'Tichka regularly experiences dense mountain fog, torrential downpours, black ice, and sudden snowstorms that temporarily close the pass.</li>
          <li><strong>Rockfall Hazards:</strong> Heavy rain can dislodge boulders from steep mountain cuttings onto the asphalt.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          When you hire a local Berber driver, they drive this mountain pass dozens of times each season. They know every blind corner, know precisely which gravel shoulders are firm, and know how to maintain vehicle cooling on 35°C summer ascents. When you self-drive, you spend four tense hours gripping the steering wheel, unable to glance at the majestic snowy peaks.
        </p>

        <h3>Hazard 2: City Traffic &amp; Roundabout "Priorité à Droite"</h3>
        <p>
          Entering Marrakech, Casablanca, or Fes during peak hours is a sensory shock for uninitiated drivers. Lanes are treated as loose suggestions. Mopeds, electric scooters, horse-drawn carriages (calèches), handcarts, stray dogs, and pedestrians in dark djellabas cross eight-lane boulevards simultaneously.
        </p>
        <p>
          Crucially, Morocco enforces an idiosyncratic French colonial traffic rule: <strong>Priorité à droite (Priority to the right)</strong>. On many older city roundabouts without explicit "Cedez le passage" (Yield) triangle signs, <em>traffic entering the roundabout has the legal right of way over vehicles already inside the circle</em>. Hesitating or slamming on brakes inside a multi-lane Marrakech roundabout is a prime cause of tourist fender-benders.
        </p>

        <h3>Hazard 3: The Strict Gendarmerie Royale Radar Net</h3>
        <p>
          Morocco is one of the most heavily policed driving environments in North Africa. Every 25 to 50 kilometers along regional roads, you will encounter a checkpoint manned by the <strong>Gendarmerie Royale</strong> (rural police) or the <strong>Sûreté Nationale</strong> (urban police).
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>The Mandatory Stop:</strong> Checkpoints are marked by signs reading <em>"Halte Police"</em> or <em>"Halte Gendarmerie"</em>. You <strong>must come to a complete, dead stop</strong> at the sign. Do not roll through. You must wait patiently until the officer makes eye contact and waves you forward with a hand gesture. Rolling through without permission can result in an immediate 400 MAD fine.</li>
          <li><strong>Laser Speed Guns:</strong> Police officers frequently conceal laser radar speed guns behind date palms, olive groves, or bridge abutments, right at the point where speed limits abruptly drop from 100 km/h to 60 km/h on village approaches.</li>
          <li><strong>On-the-Spot Fines:</strong> If caught exceeding the limit by even 7 km/h, you will be pulled over and issued a fine ranging from <strong>150 to 300 MAD</strong> (approx. €15 to €30), which must be paid immediately in cash in exchange for an official paper receipt. While not financially catastrophic, multiple stops and language barriers can sour your holiday mood.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Hazard 4: Night Driving Risks</h3>
        <p>
          Nearly every Western embassy in Rabat issues the same advisory: <strong>Avoid driving after dark on unlit rural Moroccan roads</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Rural roads lack streetlights, reflective cat-eyes, and painted lane dividers. After sunset, you will encounter pedestrians walking alongside the road dressed in dark djellabas, bicycles with zero reflectors, unlit tractors hauling hay bales, and wandering donkeys or camels that blend seamlessly into the night asphalt. Professional private drivers schedule long journeys to conclude before dusk precisely for this reason.
        </p>

