        <h2>3. The Medina Parking &amp; Baggage Conundrum</h2>
        <p>
          One critical operational reality that self-drivers overlook is that <strong>Morocco's famous riads are located deep inside centuries-old medinas where cars cannot physically enter</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you rent a car and drive to Marrakech or Fes, the following sequence occurs:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>You arrive at the outer medina wall, navigating bumper-to-bumper scooter traffic while trying to decipher street signs.</li>
          <li>You must search for a public or private parking lot (often a dusty gravel enclosure).</li>
          <li>You will be met by a <em>"Gardien de Voiture"</em>—a parking attendant wearing a reflective yellow vest who manages spaces. You must negotiate an overnight rate (typically 20 to 50 MAD per night) and often leave your car keys behind so they can shuffle vehicles like a sliding puzzle.</li>
          <li>You must unload all your luggage onto the cobblestones, find a cart porter (koussa), negotiate their fee (20–40 MAD), and walk 15 to 25 minutes through confusing labyrinthine alleys to locate your riad door.</li>
          <li>On departure day, you must repeat the entire exercise in reverse.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          In contrast, when you hire a <strong>Roam Morocco private driver</strong>:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Your driver drives directly to the nearest authorized drop-off gate.</li>
          <li>Your driver contacts your riad manager in advance via WhatsApp, ensuring riad staff are waiting curbside with baggage carts to escort you directly to reception.</li>
          <li>Your driver takes the vehicle away to secure private parking at no extra cost to you.</li>
          <li>You never worry about your car being scratched, blocked in, or broken into while you relax in a rooftop pool.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>4. The Hidden Cultural Value of a Local Berber Driver</h2>
        <p>
          Perhaps the greatest distinction between renting a car and hiring a chauffeur is not measured in Euros or Dirhams—it is measured in the <strong>depth of your Moroccan experience</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          A licensed private driver in Morocco is far more than a person behind the steering wheel. They are your <strong>cultural ambassador, translator, and local protector</strong>.
        </p>

        <!-- Cultural Benefits Grid -->
        <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); gap: 16px; margin: 32px 0;">
