        <h2>6. If You Decide to Rent a Car: 7 Essential Rules for Survival</h2>
        <p>
          If you have weighed the trade-offs and decided that self-driving matches your travel style, adhere strictly to these seven expert rules:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <strong>Book via a Reputable Aggregator with Full Excess Coverage:</strong> We recommend <a href="https://www.discovercars.com/?a_aid=578287" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DiscoverCars</a> because their Full Coverage policy refunds your deductible in the event of minor scratches or windshield cracks, which local kiosks often exploit.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Take a Full 4K Walkaround Video at Pickup:</strong> Before leaving the airport parking bay, film a continuous slow video of every body panel, wheel rim, tire sidewall, headlights, and windshield. Zoom in on existing stone chips and ensure the agent notes every single scratch on the signed inspection sheet.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Never Drive at Night:</strong> Plan all road trips so that you arrive at your riad or hotel at least 45 minutes before sunset.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Install an Airalo eSIM for Instant GPS:</strong> Do not rely on rental agency GPS units (which are often outdated). Install an affordable Morocco eSIM from <a href="https://www.airalo.com/morocco-esim" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Airalo</a> before boarding your flight, and download offline maps for the entire country on Google Maps or Maps.me.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Always Come to a Full Stop at Checkpoints:</strong> When you see <em>"Halte Police"</em> or <em>"Halte Gendarmerie"</em>, stop completely at the sign and wait for the wave.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Keep Dirham Cash in Small Denominations:</strong> Carry 10, 20, and 50 MAD notes at all times for parking guardians (gardiens de voitures), highway toll booths, and potential radar fines.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Check the Spare Tire &amp; Jack:</strong> Mountain potholes and unpaved shoulder gravel cause frequent punctures. Verify that the spare tire is properly inflated and the jack and lug wrench are present in the trunk before driving away.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2>7. If You Decide to Hire a Private Driver: How to Book Safely</h2>
        <p>
          If you opt for the safety, luxury, and cultural richness of a private driver, make sure you avoid unlicensed operators:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Verify "Transport Touristique" License Plates:</strong> In Morocco, official tourist transport vehicles bear dedicated blue or metallic registration plates indicating commercial passenger authorization. Unlicensed drivers operating personal civilian cars risk vehicle impoundment at police checkpoints, leaving passengers stranded on the highway.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Insist on Transparent All-Inclusive Quotes:</strong> Ensure in writing that your daily quote includes the vehicle, English-speaking chauffeur, fuel, highway tolls, parking fees, and the driver's overnight hotel room and meals.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Look for High-End Fleet Maintenance:</strong> Ensure your tour operator uses modern Toyota Land Cruiser Prado 4x4s or Mercedes Vito/V-Class vans with functioning dual air conditioning, pristine tires, and onboard Wi-Fi.
          </li>
        </ul>
